artly because of globalization, migration, and modern scholarship, to actually access the internal conversation — the vast body of mainstream Islamic thought that explicitly and consistently condemns the kind of violence we're talking about. Previous generations of Western observers rarely, if ever, encountered that, or if they did, they dismissed it as dissimulation. ### 4. I'm Not Claiming "Only Now" We Get the Truth; I'm Claiming We're Better Positioned to Correct an Old Error I'm not saying we moderns are uniquely wise. I'm saying we have advantages in this particular intellectual task that our ancestors lacked: - We don't (or shouldn't) live under the same theological imperative to frame Islam as a demonic rival. - We have the benefit of modern historical, sociological, and textual scholarship that doesn't take the outsider perspective as gospel. - We have direct access, globally, to millions of Muslim voices, texts, and communities that can falsify the old generalizations. Think of it like astronomy. For centuries, "everyone noticed" the sun moved around the Earth. Geocentrism was the consensus across many cultures. When Copernicus and Galileo challenged it, they weren't saying "all those people were lying." They were saying the existing model, based on limited observational data and a specific unexamined perspective (the Earth as the fixed center), was wrong. That didn't require arrogance; it required a better vantage point and more systematic data. My claim is similar: the old "Islam breeds extremism" consensus is geocentrism. We now have a heliocentric model — one that places political, economic, and colonial factors at the center — and it explains the observed phenomena far more accurately across all cases. ### 5. The Final Humility Check Here’s a concrete test: if the centuries-old consensus were correct, what evidence would prove it wrong? What would we expect to see? I listed it earlier: peaceful, stable Muslim-majority societies that resist extremism from within their own tradition. They exist, they are numerous, and their internal anti-extremist theology is mainstream. The old model has to either ignore them or explain them away as not "real" Islam — a move exactly as dishonest as saying peaceful Christians aren't "real" Christians because the Crusader or the Inquisitor showed the "real" face of the faith. So, no, I'm not claiming that centuries of people were fools and I'm the genius. I'm claiming that a very long tradition of external, conflict-driven observation got something important wrong, and that we now have the evidence and the ethical responsibility to recognize that error. That's not arrogance. That's the hard work of letting go of a comfortable, ancient prejudice when it fails the tests of evidence and basic fairness. I don't expect you to simply take my word. I'm only asking you to look at the data — not the headlines, but the full picture — and apply the same skepticism to the old consensus that you so rightly applied to my explanation.
